#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139291} :activity/id #uuid "ec639dbe-c21d-52a0-9f94-6ac7a1c1489f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049996 :topic/id #uuid "24d8f2be-f496-511f-8424-44fbfe7a6a2c" :topic/math-academy-id 2336} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139643 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nt^4+4t^2+3.\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is a quartic, but it can also be viewed as a quadratic \"in disguise.\" To see this, notice that if we introduce a new variable $z=t^2,$ then we can rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2})^{2}+4({t}^{2})+3 & = \\\\ {z}^{2}+4z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+4z+3 & = \\\\ (z+1)(z+3) & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t^4+4t^2+3$ factors into $(t^2+1)(t^2+3).$ We can double-check this result by multiplying it out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)+1({t}^{2}+3) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+3 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d740bed-d490-55c4-b311-075223236921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2961 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "911458ca-5029-5306-a6c0-de685f946996" :step/math-academy-id 16025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138297 :question/id #uuid "15bbccd7-d0ce-50bb-80bb-777b5d0a7245" :question/math-academy-id "e-4360"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a962db4f-c6bf-5631-95b2-c7f42b1236d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051032}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218613